lesson "Differentiation IV — Trigonometric Functions" {
  course: "calculus"
  skills: ["deriv-trig-sin-cos", "deriv-trig-other"]

  slide "The Star Result" {
    id: "d4-intro"
    cat: "Sine & Cosine"
    > Here is one of the most beautiful facts in calculus:
    >
    > $$\frac{d}{dx}\,\sin x = \cos x$$
    >
    > The slope of the sine curve, at every point, is exactly the **height of the
    > cosine curve**. Drag the point below and watch it: where sine is steepest
    > (at $x = 0$) its slope is $1 = \cos 0$; at the crest of sine the slope is
    > $0 = \cos(\tfrac{\pi}{2})$.

    scene plane {
      x: [-6.5, 6.5]
      y: [-1.6, 1.6]
      grid
      axes
      param a = 0.6 { range: [-6.2, 6.2], step: 0.1 }
      curve s = sin(x) { color: primary, width: 3 }
      curve c = cos(x) { color: accent, width: 2, style: dashed }
      point P = (a, sin(a)) { color: primary, r: 7, drag: x -> a }
      point S = (a, cos(a)) { color: accent, r: 5, label: "slope = ${cos(a)}" }
      line tangent { through: P, slope: cos(a), color: success, width: 2 }
      slider a { label: "a" }
    }
  }

  slide "And Its Partner" {
    id: "d4-cos"
    cat: "Sine & Cosine"
    > Cosine differentiates into sine — but watch the **sign**:
    >
    > $$\frac{d}{dx}\,\cos x = -\sin x$$
    >
    > The minus sign appears because cosine is *falling* where sine is positive.
    > These two derivatives are the seed for every other trig rule.
  }

  slide "Sketch Cosine's Derivative" {
    id: "d4-sketch"
    cat: "Sine & Cosine"
    > $\cos x$ is shown below. Sketch its derivative, $-\sin x$, before moving on.

    scene plane {
      x: [-6.5, 6.5]
      y: [-1.6, 1.6]
      grid
      axes
      curve c = cos(x) { color: primary, width: 3 }
    }

    sketch curve {
      ask "Draw the derivative of cos x."
      skill: "deriv-trig-sin-cos"
      near (0, 0)
      near (1.5708, -1)
      near (3.1416, 0)
      near (-1.5708, 1)
      tol: 0.4
      hint "The derivative of cos x is -sin x."
      ! "The derivative of cos x is -sin x — zero at 0 and π, at its lowest at π/2."
    }
  }

  slide "The Other Four" {
    id: "d4-table"
    cat: "All Six"
    > From $\sin$ and $\cos$ (via the quotient rule) come the rest. Notice the
    > pattern: every **co**-function ($\cos,\ \cot,\ \csc$) carries a **minus sign**.
    >
    > $$\frac{d}{dx}\,\tan x = \sec^{2} x \qquad \frac{d}{dx}\,\cot x = -\csc^{2} x$$
    >
    > $$\frac{d}{dx}\,\sec x = \sec x\,\tan x \qquad \frac{d}{dx}\,\csc x = -\csc x\,\cot x$$

    scene plane {
      x: [0.2, 1.3]
      y: [-2, 5]
      grid
      axes
      choice fn = "tan" { options: ["tan", "cot", "sec", "csc"] }
      curve ftan = tan(x) { color: primary, width: 3, show: fn == "tan" }
      curve dtan = (1 / cos(x))^2 { color: accent, width: 2, style: dashed, show: fn == "tan" }
      curve fcot = 1 / tan(x) { color: primary, width: 3, show: fn == "cot" }
      curve dcot = 0 - (1 / sin(x))^2 { color: accent, width: 2, style: dashed, show: fn == "cot" }
      curve fsec = 1 / cos(x) { color: primary, width: 3, show: fn == "sec" }
      curve dsec = (1 / cos(x)) * tan(x) { color: accent, width: 2, style: dashed, show: fn == "sec" }
      curve fcsc = 1 / sin(x) { color: primary, width: 3, show: fn == "csc" }
      curve dcsc = 0 - (1 / sin(x)) * (1 / tan(x)) { color: accent, width: 2, style: dashed, show: fn == "csc" }
      picker fn { label: "pick a function to graph, with its derivative dashed" }
    }
  }

  slide "Tangent and Its Derivative" {
    id: "d4-tan"
    cat: "All Six"
    > Between its vertical asymptotes, $\tan x$ (blue) only ever climbs — never
    > flat, never falling. That matches its derivative $\sec^{2}x$ (dashed), which
    > is **always positive**. Drag the point to feel how steep $\tan$ gets near the
    > edges.

    scene plane {
      x: [-1.4, 1.4]
      y: [-6, 6]
      grid
      axes
      param a = 0.5 { range: [-1.4, 1.4], step: 0.05 }
      curve t = tan(x) { color: primary, width: 3 }
      curve dt = 1 / (cos(x))^2 { color: accent, width: 2, style: dashed }
      point P = (a, tan(a)) { color: primary, r: 7, drag: x -> a }
      line tangent { through: P, slope: 1 / (cos(a))^2, color: success, width: 2 }
      slider a { label: "a" }
    }
  }

  slide "Derivative of Cosine" {
    id: "d4-quiz-cos"
    cat: "Check"
    quiz {
      ask "What is $\frac{d}{dx}\,\cos x$?"
      skill: "deriv-trig-sin-cos"
      - "$\sin x$" { why: "Almost — cosine's derivative carries a minus sign." }
      * "$-\sin x$"
      - "$\cos x$"
      - "$-\cos x$"
      hint "Cosine is falling where sine is positive."
      ! "$\frac{d}{dx}\cos x = -\sin x$."
    }
  }

  slide "Derivative of Tangent" {
    id: "d4-quiz-tan"
    cat: "Check"
    quiz {
      ask "What is $\frac{d}{dx}\,\tan x$?"
      skill: "deriv-trig-other"
      * "$\sec^2 x$"
      - "$-\csc^2 x$" { why: "That's the derivative of cot x, the co-function." }
      - "$\sec x \tan x$" { why: "That's the derivative of sec x." }
      - "$\cot x$"
      hint "Write tan as sin/cos and apply the quotient rule."
      ! "$\frac{d}{dx}\tan x = \sec^2 x$."
    }
  }

  slide "Chain Rule Meets Sine" {
    id: "d4-num"
    cat: "Check"
    numeric {
      ask "Let $h(x) = \sin(2x)$. Find $h'(0)$."
      skill: "deriv-trig-sin-cos"
      answer: 2
      hint "Chain rule: $h'(x) = \cos(2x)\cdot 2$."
      ! "$h'(x) = 2\cos(2x)$, so $h'(0) = 2\cos 0 = 2$."
    }
  }

  slide "Build a Trig Derivative" {
    id: "d4-build"
    cat: "Check"
    build {
      ask "Assemble $\frac{d}{dx}\,\sec x$."
      skill: "deriv-trig-other"
      bank: ["sec x", "·", "tan x", "csc x", "-"]
      answer: ["sec x", "·", "tan x"]
      hint "It's a product of two trig functions, no minus sign."
      ! "$\frac{d}{dx}\sec x = \sec x\,\tan x$."
    }
  }
}
